+# LDN relay multiplayer (experimental)
+
+LDNR lets a game using `nn::ldn` local wireless and the virtual `nn::socket`
+API play through a separate `ldn-relay-server`. It is opt-in. With LDNR off,
+symbol lookup and all offline behavior are unchanged.
+
+## Configure
+
+Set these in the NX2Apple environment before launching the game:
+
+| Variable | Meaning |
+| --- | --- |
+| `DM_LDNR=1` | Enable the LDNR symbol table. Any other value leaves the upstream offline hooks in charge. |
+| `DM_LDNR_SERVER=host:11470` | Relay TCP endpoint. The port defaults to 11470 when omitted. Bracket IPv6 literals, for example `[2001:db8::1]:11470`. |
+| `DM_LDNR_ROOM_PASSWORD=...` | Optional relay-room password. Every player must use the same value. This is separate from the game's LDN passphrase. |
+| `DM_LDNR_ALLOW_PLAINTEXT_PASSWORD=1` | Allow sending `DM_LDNR_ROOM_PASSWORD` over the plaintext transport. Without it the client refuses a password room locally, before anything is sent. |
+| `DM_LDNR_NAME=...` | Optional player/room display name, truncated to the native 32-byte name field. |
+| `DM_LDNR_LOG=1` | Log relay lifecycle and socket errors. Zero or unset disables logging. |
+
+Start the relay separately and make its TCP port reachable by every player.
+The client speaks protocol 1.2 and requests UDP, TCP, broadcast, advertise
+updates, and password-room support. It refuses servers that do not negotiate
+the required data-plane capabilities.
+
+**The built-in transport is plaintext TCP: it has no relay authentication
+and no integrity protection.** Traffic to and from the relay is readable and
+modifiable by anyone on the path, and nothing proves the endpoint is the
+relay you intended. Do not use a reusable password. When the relay crosses
+an untrusted network, run the connection through a TLS tunnel or a VPN — the
+relay server supports TLS — so the plaintext client transport is never
+exposed. A TLS-enabled relay endpoint cannot be used directly until
+certificate-verifying TLS support is added to the client; an authenticated,
+integrity-protected transport is a known follow-up.
+
+**Room passwords require TLS on the relay by default.** Until client TLS
+exists, a relay that enforces this refuses a password room over plaintext
+(the join fails with the distinct authentication-protocol error). The client
+itself refuses to send a room password over plaintext with the same error,
+before any byte leaves the machine. For a trusted network or VPN, set
+`DM_LDNR_ALLOW_PLAINTEXT_PASSWORD=1` on every player and start the relay with
+`LDNR_REQUIRE_TLS_FOR_PASSWORD=false`. Without a room password neither is
+needed. The game's LDN SecurityConfig
+passphrase is not affected by this: it is part of the LDN protocol, not the
+relay.
+
+## Network model
+
+A relay room is one LDN network. The host is normally `10.114.0.1`; stations
+receive addresses through `10.114.0.8`, with mask `255.255.0.0`. The relay
+assigns native network/security information and per-node addresses. Scanning,
+joining, advertise data, accept policy/filter updates, rejection, disconnects,
+and host destruction update the native cached state and signal the attached
+LDN state-change event.
+
+UDP and basic TCP sockets use virtual descriptors, not host descriptors.
+Datagrams and stream segments travel only between members of the room.
+Directed broadcast `10.114.255.255` and limited broadcast `255.255.255.255`
+are supported. Blocking guest waits use the host's native-wait helper.
+
+LDNR deliberately does **not** provide general internet access:
+
+- Destinations outside the virtual LDN subnet fail with `ENETDOWN` (or the
+  existing offline `-1` result at the glue boundary).
+- Resolver, `eventfd`, `recvmsg`, and `sendmsg` mappings remain unavailable.
+- NIFM is not overridden; the existing conservative offline NIFM behavior is
+  unchanged.
+- TCP is an emulated in-room stream, with simple connect/accept/data/FIN
+  handling. It is not a full host TCP stack and has no internet routing,
+  congestion control, or TLS.
+- UDP payloads are limited to 1500 bytes and relayed TCP chunks to 16 KiB.
+  Relay rate limits and queue limits also apply.
+
+## Tests
+
+The portable tests build on POSIX hosts without the Apple SDK:
+
+```sh
+make -C dm/host/ldnr/tests run
+```
+
+The E2E test exits successfully with a `SKIP` message unless
+`LDNR_SERVER_BIN` points to a relay executable. To build and run it against
+the separate relay checkout:
+
+```sh
+cd /workspace/ldn-relay-server
+go build -o /tmp/ldn-relay-server ./cmd/ldn-relay-server
+cd /workspace/NX2Apple/engine
+LDNR_SERVER_BIN=/tmp/ldn-relay-server make -C dm/host/ldnr/tests run
+```
+
+The E2E starts its own loopback server and exercises host/join, filtered scan
+and advertise data, two nodes, UDP unicast/broadcast, TCP in both directions,
+disconnect, reconnect, and host-destroy notification. The test target also
+performs a Linux `-fsyntax-only` check of the Apple glue with shim headers;
+it is not a substitute for an Xcode build or on-device gameplay testing.
